<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Late antiquity</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 250–<a href="/years/750">750</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Spätantike</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Late antiquity is the period between classical antiquity and the Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> world, parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, roughly from c. 250 to 750, though its exact limits are debated. It saw the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>, and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, Rabbinic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> were canonised. The term was used by German-speaking historians after Alois Riegl and given currency in English by Peter Brown, whose The World of Late Antiquity of 1971 replaced the view of a declining culture with one of renewal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/late-antiquity.jpg" alt="Late antiquity" width="330" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original Uploader was Sigune at {}. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vergilius_romanus_234v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term Spätantike has been used by German-speaking historians since its popularisation by Alois Riegl in the early 20th century. Peter Brown gave it currency in English: his The World of Late Antiquity (1971) revised the Gibbon view of a stale classical culture in favour of a vibrant time of renewals. Writers who stress continuity point to the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> as reorganised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>. The names &quot;Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>&quot; and &quot;Early Byzantine&quot; instead emphasise a break with the classical past, and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/migration-period">Migration Period</a>&quot; plays down the disruption caused by Germanic kingdoms within Roman borders, beginning with the foedus with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Aquitania</a> in 418.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, who began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a> of four emperors, the replacement of the Parthian rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> led to the Roman-Sasanian Wars. Constantine the Great made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> legal with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-milan">Edict of Milan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a> the state church. By the 6th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> had ten times the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/migration-period">Migration Period</a> brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-410">Sack of Rome</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> in 410 and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> in 455, and by 476 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> had fallen.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> appeared in the 7th century, and Arab armies invaded the Eastern Roman and Sasanian empires, destroying the latter. Historians disagree on its place: the Pirenne Thesis makes the Arab invasions the end of late antiquity, while scholars in the tradition of Peter Brown see Islam as a product of the late antique world. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> fell from 800,000 people at the start of the period to 30,000 at its end.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Late antique art marks the end of classical Roman art and the beginning of medieval art, moving from idealised realism to a stylised manner that hints at a spiritual reality behind its subjects, as in the Portrait of the Four Tetrarchs of about 300. In literature the period saw the rise of literary cultures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, Armenian, Georgian, Ethiopic, Arabic and Coptic, and an explosion of Christian writing by Eusebius of Caesarea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-of-caesarea">Basil of Caesarea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose of Milan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a>. Its architecture includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> in the sixth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Late antiquity” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
